Top Takeaways for Beginner Investors

Getting Started: Keep It Simple, Start Small & Build Habits
Dimitri kept coming back to this: investing is a long-term journey. Start early, even with small amounts, using recurring investments (dollar-cost averaging) to smooth out market ups and downs. Don't get paralyzed by too many choices—focus on diversification and consistency.
GoSmart is RBC's tool made for exactly this stage:
Commission-free automated recurring investments in all-in-one ETFs (perfect for set-it-and-forget-it beginners).
50 commission-free trades per year on stocks and ETFs.
53 always-free ETFs (including options for crypto and gold exposure).
No account fees, no minimums.
Instant fund transfers if you're already with RBC (start trading in minutes, no delays or hidden friction).
Canadian-based support when you need it.
It's streamlined in the RBC Mobile app and geared toward registered accounts like TFSA, RRSP, and FHSA in CAD. Great for new investors who want to get going without needing to be experts.

RBC Direct Investing's Approach vs. Other Options
One of the most upvoted questions (576 upvotes) was about why choose RBC. Dimitri highlighted the stability: 150+ years of experience, rock-solid infrastructure, deep expertise, and trusted support for millions of clients worldwide.
He said GoSmart was created to meet new and aspiring investors where they are—removing cost hurdles so beginners can focus on learning and growing. For more established investors with complex needs, RBC offers premium execution and tools backed by that long history.
He praised the innovation in the Canadian investing space but raised thoughtful points about models that might encourage too much trading (which can hurt long-term results). RBC's goal: help beginners build the right habits early.
Commissions & "Free" Trading Explained
A big question (255 upvotes) was about commissions and why not go fully zero-commission everywhere. Dimitri explained that "free" trading isn't truly zero-cost—there are often hidden elements like FX fees or other charges.
More importantly, models built on very high trading volume can push overtrading, which studies show often leads to worse returns. RBC's approach:
GoSmart removes barriers for beginners with commission-free recurring ETF investments and a generous set of free trades/ETFs to get you started and learning.
On the full platform, commissions help fund high-quality support and execution for investors with bigger or more advanced needs.
No plans for broad commission cuts soon—the focus is on making the entry point easy via GoSmart.

More Beginner-Friendly Advice from the Thread
Prioritize registered accounts (TFSA/RRSP/FHSA) based on your life stage—many asked, but it's personal.
Use broad-market ETFs for easy diversification and long-term growth.
Volatility is normal—stick to your plan instead of trying to time the market.
Features like fractional shares are valued for accessibility (e.g., investing every dollar), but no specific updates were shared.
RBC is serious about growing its share of early-stage investors, so expect more beginner-focused tools ahead.


